WEBVTT

00:00:00.295 --> 00:00:03.442
UPDATE changes values in rows that already exist.

00:00:03.442 --> 00:00:07.027
The safest pattern is to preview the intended target first.

00:00:07.027 --> 00:00:11.497
In this example, the SELECT and UPDATE use the same key-based condition.

00:00:11.497 --> 00:00:13.737
Run the cell and watch the sequence.

00:00:13.737 --> 00:00:15.487
First identify the row. Then change it.

00:00:15.487 --> 00:00:16.612
Finally inspect what PostgreSQL returns.

00:00:16.612 --> 00:00:22.927
A unique medical record number is safer than a patient name because names can repeat or change.

00:00:22.927 --> 00:00:25.122
When writing UPDATE, treat WHERE as a safety boundary.

00:00:25.122 --> 00:00:28.612
Before you execute, ask how many rows you expect the condition to match.

00:00:28.612 --> 00:00:32.862
If the answer is one row, but your preview SELECT returns several,

00:00:32.862 --> 00:00:34.577
stop and fix the predicate before running the update.

00:00:34.577 --> 00:00:39.752
This preview-then-update pattern is one of the most useful habits in practical SQL work.

00:00:39.752 --> 00:00:41.742
Writes deserve a slower workflow than reads.

00:00:41.742 --> 00:00:48.032
Before UPDATE or DELETE, run the intended WHERE condition as a SELECT and inspect the affected rows.

00:00:48.032 --> 00:00:52.387
In production, use transactions when appropriate so you can verify the change before committing.

00:00:52.387 --> 00:00:57.120
The absence of a syntax error does not mean the correct records were modified.
